WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.966
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4530.

2
00:00:00.966 --> 00:00:01.932
Begin Session II.]

3
00:00:01.932 --> 00:00:07.085
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Alright, today is October first, the first day of October— that's exciting—and a beautiful Thursday afternoon.

4
00:00:07.085 --> 00:00:14.169
I'm here with . . . This is Chelsea Arseneault with the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

5
00:00:14.169 --> 00:00:21.254
I'm here today with Ms. Gloria Rogers in her beautiful home in Lake Charles [Louisiana] to talk about her memories of Mossville,

6
00:00:21.254 --> 00:00:27.695
Louisiana as part of a project conducted in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville.

7
00:00:27.695 --> 00:00:33.814
This is our second interview together, our first one being at the oral history luncheon on September twenty fourth.

8
00:00:33.814 --> 00:00:38.000
And you were talking about [A.M.] Barbe High School [Lake Charles, LA].

9
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:38.595
<v GLORIA ROGERS>Okay.

10
00:00:38.595 --> 00:00:39.189
<v ARSENEAULT>B-A-R-B-E?

11
00:00:39.189 --> 00:00:39.784
<v ROGERS>[Agrees]

12
00:00:39.784 --> 00:00:40.378
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

13
00:00:40.378 --> 00:00:41.568
<v ROGERS>Excuse me.

14
00:00:41.568 --> 00:00:43.946
Could you pause that?

15
00:00:43.946 --> 00:00:47.514
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah, of course. [break in tape]

16
00:00:47.514 --> 00:00:48.703
<v ROGERS>. . . High School.

17
00:00:48.703 --> 00:00:49.297
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

18
00:00:49.297 --> 00:00:52.270
<v ROGERS>When I first went there . . .

19
00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:54.649
<v ARSENEAULT>To Mossville High School?

20
00:00:54.649 --> 00:00:55.243
<v ROGERS>Yes.

21
00:00:55.243 --> 00:00:58.811
That was in August of 1955.

22
00:00:58.811 --> 00:01:00.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

23
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.892
<v ROGERS>The students were curious.

24
00:01:01.892 --> 00:01:04.730
Oh, Lord they were so curious.

25
00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:09.459
And they would make remarks—not that the remarks were bad.

26
00:01:09.459 --> 00:01:16.554
But then after they got into my class, some of their attitudes began to change,

27
00:01:16.554 --> 00:01:22.230
because they saw . . . even though at that time I was really young.

28
00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:28.378
In fact, I was as young or younger than some of my students,

29
00:01:28.378 --> 00:01:36.419
because they had not been in a situation where they could go to school all the time.

30
00:01:36.419 --> 00:01:37.365
You know?

31
00:01:37.365 --> 00:01:46.824
They did have an elementary school down in Westlake [Louisiana], and I think they might have had one in Mossville.

32
00:01:46.824 --> 00:01:48.716
I really don't know.

33
00:01:48.716 --> 00:01:52.500
But the bottom line was, because of circumstances,

34
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:04.324
a lot of them told me that they would ride the bus to Lake Charles to go to a high school called Washington High School.

35
00:02:04.324 --> 00:02:10.473
But it was just so inconvenient catching the bus, good weather, bad weather.

36
00:02:10.473 --> 00:02:10.946
And

37
00:02:10.946 --> 00:02:16.622
<c.p>as a result, they were not able to remain in high school.

38
00:02:16.622 --> 00:02:20.878
But after they built the high school in Mossville,

39
00:02:20.878 --> 00:02:32.703
the situations . . . circumstances were better because the school board provided buses to go down in Westlake and pick them up and bring them to Mossville.

40
00:02:32.703 --> 00:02:43.108
And of course, they did the same thing for the students in Vinton [Louisiana], and Vinton is twenty-something miles from over there.

41
00:02:43.108 --> 00:02:49.257
And then, they would pick . . . I believe I talked about this early on.

42
00:02:49.257 --> 00:02:56.351
The students who would come from Vinton, they would ride the bus and, of course,

43
00:02:56.351 --> 00:03:04.865
they would stop in Sulphur [Louisiana] and pick up the students and bring them to Mossville High School.

44
00:03:04.865 --> 00:03:14.324
Some of those students had the intelligence, but they had not been taught certain things that they should have known.

45
00:03:14.324 --> 00:03:20.000
As a result, a few of them had to really struggle.

46
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:25.476
But what I admired about them, they were so conscientious to learn,

47
00:03:25.476 --> 00:03:36.885
because they had been indoctrinated with the idea that if you are going to go anywhere in this life, you got to get an education.

48
00:03:36.885 --> 00:03:41.448
And they came to school with that attitude—most of them.

49
00:03:41.448 --> 00:03:46.925
You always have a few who want to do things their way.

50
00:03:46.925 --> 00:03:56.964
But those kinds were not able to survive, because it was so few of them and they didn't want to be ostracized.

51
00:03:56.964 --> 00:04:01.528
So they began to do better, even in the classroom.

52
00:04:01.528 --> 00:04:08.829
There was one young lady in my class, she . . . how would I want to describe her?

53
00:04:08.829 --> 00:04:16.587
She was, I guess, of average intelligence, but sometimes it was difficult for her to catch on.

54
00:04:16.587 --> 00:04:21.607
She would ask me if I could help her after school.

55
00:04:21.607 --> 00:04:22.976
I said, "Sure.

56
00:04:22.976 --> 00:04:30.278
If we didn't have to have any teachers meeting, I would be glad to help you."

57
00:04:30.278 --> 00:04:34.841
And that young lady was so determined to do well.

58
00:04:34.841 --> 00:04:40.317
She graduated from high school, and she went to school at Grambling.

59
00:04:40.317 --> 00:04:42.143
That's in north Louisiana.

60
00:04:42.143 --> 00:04:53.552
And do you know, she graduated from there and then she went to some university, a college, in Houston [Texas], and she graduated from there.

61
00:04:53.552 --> 00:04:55.377
She got her master's.

62
00:04:55.377 --> 00:05:06.786
So it lets me know that if a student is considered below average, if they are determined to succeed, they can, but they got to

63
00:05:06.786 --> 00:05:07.698
<c.p>want to.

64
00:05:07.698 --> 00:05:15.000
And then they would seek the help of those who were able to help them.

65
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:21.311
But some of the students, they knew they needed help, but they were somewhat shy.

66
00:05:21.311 --> 00:05:26.361
They didn't want the other students to know that they didn't know.

67
00:05:26.361 --> 00:05:35.617
And you try to tell them, "You are working for you, not for them, because if you get out of high school,

68
00:05:35.617 --> 00:05:44.033
it means that you're going to be on your own and you can't look back at your classmates and say,

69
00:05:44.033 --> 00:05:51.607
'I would have done this, I would have done that.'" But most of the students that we taught,

70
00:05:51.607 --> 00:05:55.393
they did well because they were determined to succeed.

71
00:05:55.393 --> 00:06:04.650
Those . . . There were a few who could have done better, but their attitudes were not as, I thought, they should have been.

72
00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:11.803
But you still want them to succeed, because reality check—when they get out there in this society,

73
00:06:11.803 --> 00:06:17.694
they're not only competing with their classmates; they're competing with people from all over.

74
00:06:17.694 --> 00:06:25.268
And if you're not prepared, and especially—and, of course, that was years ago— especially in the society today,

75
00:06:25.268 --> 00:06:32.000
what kind of job can you get other than maybe some of these hamburger places?

76
00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:40.789
It's nothing wrong about that, because I was telling some students . . . they were saying, "I wouldn't do this, and I wouldn't do that."

77
00:06:40.789 --> 00:06:43.463
I said, "Let me tell you something."

78
00:06:43.463 --> 00:06:47.667
I said, "You know, my husband is working and I'm working."

79
00:06:47.667 --> 00:06:51.106
And I said, "My husband went to the military.

80
00:06:51.106 --> 00:06:53.398
He stayed there over twenty years.

81
00:06:53.398 --> 00:06:55.309
He retired from the military.

82
00:06:55.309 --> 00:07:01.423
And when he got out, he was fortunate enough to get a job at Olin Chemicals."

83
00:07:01.423 --> 00:07:07.919
And I said, "Do you know he seeks overtime, in addition to what he's supposed to work.

84
00:07:07.919 --> 00:07:12.122
He's always begging whoever the head man is about working overtime."

85
00:07:12.122 --> 00:07:20.528
I said, "My point to you is this: if you have a family, and if you are trying to buy a house,

86
00:07:20.528 --> 00:07:29.317
you know. . . "I said, "You sacrifice and you do what you need to do to get it, as long as it is legitimate."

87
00:07:29.317 --> 00:07:34.667
I said, "Now, if you get into the drug society, then you are doomed.

88
00:07:34.667 --> 00:07:42.691
You might succeed for a few days or a few weeks," I said, "but it will eventually catch up with you."

89
00:07:42.691 --> 00:07:49.569
And, of course, they didn't like to hear that, but I was telling them, I said, "You know

90
00:07:49.569 --> 00:07:49.951
<c.p>what?

91
00:07:49.951 --> 00:07:58.740
If I needed a job, I would go to Burger King, McDonald's, and if they would hire me, I would accept that job."

92
00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:01.415
"Oh, I wouldn't work for three dollars."

93
00:08:01.415 --> 00:08:05.236
At the time, I don't know what they pay now.

94
00:08:05.236 --> 00:08:09.439
At the time it was three dollars and something an hour.

95
00:08:09.439 --> 00:08:10.967
I said, "I would."

96
00:08:10.967 --> 00:08:13.260
I said, "Because you know what?

97
00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:17.846
I would be able to buy bread and a few other things."

98
00:08:17.846 --> 00:08:19.756
And I said, "That's legitimate."

99
00:08:19.756 --> 00:08:22.813
I said, "You know, nothing bad lasts forever.

100
00:08:22.813 --> 00:08:27.780
So you do what you got to do to try to move on."

101
00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:31.220
You know, but they didn't want to hear it.

102
00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:34.276
Some of them, those boys especially, "Ms. Rogers,

103
00:08:34.276 --> 00:08:40.390
I can't believe you would work for three . . ." I think it was three twenty-five, or three-something.

104
00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:42.301
I said, "I surely would.

105
00:08:42.301 --> 00:08:46.122
If I needed the job, I would work for that."

106
00:08:46.122 --> 00:08:53.000
But some of their attitudes at that time were not what I thought it should have been.

107
00:08:53.000 --> 00:09:01.291
But back to Mossville, our students, a lot of them . . . well, with integration, it brought about changes.

108
00:09:01.291 --> 00:09:10.071
I'll never forget, there were students at our school who, if they had remained at Mossville High School,

109
00:09:10.071 --> 00:09:20.313
they would have been either valedictorian or salutatorian, but when they merged . . . And that was one thing those students didn't like.

110
00:09:20.313 --> 00:09:31.043
But when they would merge, there was no way that they could come into another school and be salutatorian or the valedictorian.

111
00:09:31.043 --> 00:09:32.994
And that was upsetting.

112
00:09:32.994 --> 00:09:39.822
Now, what happened, they did indicate students who had maintained a certain grade average.

113
00:09:39.822 --> 00:09:45.675
They honored them by putting an asterisk by their names for graduation.

114
00:09:45.675 --> 00:09:48.113
Students are graduating with honor.

115
00:09:48.113 --> 00:09:55.429
But like one of the boys said, "Well Ms. Rogers, I would have been valedictorian."

116
00:09:55.429 --> 00:09:57.868
I said, "True, true, true."

117
00:09:57.868 --> 00:10:00.794
I said, “But you know what?

118
00:10:00.794 --> 00:10:03.721
The best is yet to come.

119
00:10:03.721 --> 00:10:04.696
Hang on!

120
00:10:04.696 --> 00:10:05.672
Hang on!

121
00:10:05.672 --> 00:10:06.647
Hang on!"

122
00:10:06.647 --> 00:10:15.426
"Ms. Rogers . . .” You know, they're going to show me where I didn't know what I was talking about.

123
00:10:15.426 --> 00:10:18.840
I said, "Look, nothing bad lasts forever.

124
00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:19.328
Nothing.

125
00:10:19.328 --> 00:10:28.595
Because, according to . . ." They didn't like to hear me say this, but I think it was Ralph Waldo Emerson,

126
00:10:28.595 --> 00:10:35.911
in a poem that he had written, he said, "In life, there is the balancing."

127
00:10:35.911 --> 00:10:39.325
And I have come to believe that.

128
00:10:39.325 --> 00:10:40.788
You might be

129
00:10:40.788 --> 00:10:51.031
<c.p>down today, but if you want to move up with God's help, you can move up, but you got to work.

130
00:10:51.031 --> 00:10:59.322
And nobody's just going to say, "Because you're a minority, we're going to give you a job."

131
00:10:59.322 --> 00:11:02.736
No, they're not going to do that.

132
00:11:02.736 --> 00:11:11.028
And like I would tell my students, you got to show that you are really, really concerned.

133
00:11:11.028 --> 00:11:15.905
But when I reflect on the students that I taught,

134
00:11:15.905 --> 00:11:23.221
they . . . most of them— there are exceptions to any rule—they have done well in life.

135
00:11:23.221 --> 00:11:28.586
The young man who did that portrait, that picture of me . . .

136
00:11:28.586 --> 00:11:30.049
<v ARSENEAULT>That's your picture?

137
00:11:30.049 --> 00:11:30.537
<v ROGERS>[Agrees]

138
00:11:30.537 --> 00:11:32.000
<v ARSENEAULT>That's good.

139
00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:32.498
<v ROGERS>Yeah.

140
00:11:32.498 --> 00:11:35.982
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have to sit for it?

141
00:11:35.982 --> 00:11:36.479
<v ROGERS>Yes.

142
00:11:36.479 --> 00:11:49.419
No, what happened, they were getting ready to have school reunion, but at this reunion they wanted all of the black high schools in the area—Mossville,

143
00:11:49.419 --> 00:11:55.392
Washington Marion, LaGrange, all of the black schools—they wanted to have homecoming.

144
00:11:55.392 --> 00:12:01.862
So they . . . those students from Mossville selected me to be the parade marshal.

145
00:12:01.862 --> 00:12:04.848
Okay, but they wanted a picture.

146
00:12:04.848 --> 00:12:17.788
And I gave one of the students a picture, but they didn't tell me that they were sending it to the young man who's an artist.

147
00:12:17.788 --> 00:12:19.779
He lives in California.

148
00:12:19.779 --> 00:12:23.263
They said, "We're sending it after the

149
00:12:23.263 --> 00:12:23.760
<c.p>fact."

150
00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:28.240
They told me they had sent it to him.

151
00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:31.724
His name was . . . last name was Collier.

152
00:12:31.724 --> 00:12:33.714
He was from Sulphur.

153
00:12:33.714 --> 00:12:37.198
But in high school he always drew.

154
00:12:37.198 --> 00:12:39.189
That was his thing.

155
00:12:39.189 --> 00:12:47.650
If he . . . Like in class, and you're supposed to be concentrating on this, doing this, he's drawing.

156
00:12:47.650 --> 00:12:50.636
I mean, that was his thing.

157
00:12:50.636 --> 00:13:00.590
And he went to California—I don't remember which part of California—and he got a job somewhere at an art something,

158
00:13:00.590 --> 00:13:06.562
and it helped to develop his . . . it helped to develop his talents.

159
00:13:06.562 --> 00:13:20.000
So, they . . . some of the students sent the picture to him and he drew that picture over there from the one that was sent to him.

160
00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:30.882
And then I had another student who told me, she said, "Ms. Rogers, you remember how you used to have us do those book reports?"

161
00:13:30.882 --> 00:13:40.024
They had to read the books, and they had to discuss them based on certain criteria that I'd given to them.

162
00:13:40.024 --> 00:13:48.294
She said, "I went to California and," she said, "I decided I was going to go back to school,

163
00:13:48.294 --> 00:13:52.647
get my master's, and do whatever I needed to do."

164
00:13:52.647 --> 00:14:01.788
And she said, "When I was called in for an interview to become principal at one high school . . . " She told me,

165
00:14:01.788 --> 00:14:03.529
but I don't remember.

166
00:14:03.529 --> 00:14:10.494
She said the people who were questioning her they told her, "Oh, you really did well.

167
00:14:10.494 --> 00:14:12.671
You just speak so well."

168
00:14:12.671 --> 00:14:17.024
She said, "Ms. Rogers, you know what I told them?

169
00:14:17.024 --> 00:14:18.765
I have no idea."

170
00:14:18.765 --> 00:14:27.471
She said, "I told them I had this English teacher . . .” Every month, all of them, they had to report because,

171
00:14:27.471 --> 00:14:37.047
you see, some of those students would go somewhere and get somebody to tell them something, and they would write that down.

172
00:14:37.047 --> 00:14:45.318
They didn't know I had this book that had all of the books in there, summarized . . . the main points.

173
00:14:45.318 --> 00:14:49.235
And they would come up there with some foolishness.

174
00:14:49.235 --> 00:14:51.847
I would ask the other students.

175
00:14:51.847 --> 00:14:57.071
I said . . . Because the students evaluated what the other students were doing.

176
00:14:57.071 --> 00:15:01.859
And one girl got up there and she just said anything.

177
00:15:01.859 --> 00:15:07.082
She gave the title of the book, and she gave the author.

178
00:15:07.082 --> 00:15:12.306
When she finished, the students said, "Ms. Rogers, is that the truth?"

179
00:15:12.306 --> 00:15:14.918
I said, "You ask the presenter."

180
00:15:14.918 --> 00:15:17.094
She had to come clean.

181
00:15:17.094 --> 00:15:21.012
She said, "Oh, I thought I could get over."

182
00:15:21.012 --> 00:15:21.447
I

183
00:15:21.447 --> 00:15:29.282
<c.p>said, "What do you think?" [laughs] But anyway, she got a U [a failing grade] for that too.

184
00:15:29.282 --> 00:15:31.024
She would tease me.

185
00:15:31.024 --> 00:15:34.941
She said, "Ms. Rogers, you remember the book report?"

186
00:15:34.941 --> 00:15:38.424
I said, "Yes, because it was so different.

187
00:15:38.424 --> 00:15:46.259
You getting up there and giving the title of the book and the author and just saying anything."

188
00:15:46.259 --> 00:15:50.176
And I said, "I gave you what you earned."

189
00:15:50.176 --> 00:15:51.482
"Oh, Ms. Rogers."

190
00:15:51.482 --> 00:15:58.882
But anyway, it was all in fun because she is out of school and all of that.

191
00:15:58.882 --> 00:16:02.365
But back to the young lady in California.

192
00:16:02.365 --> 00:16:08.894
She said, "Ms. Rogers,” she said, “when you would have us do those book reports,"

193
00:16:08.894 --> 00:16:14.988
she said, "I would really become frustrated because I didn't want to do it."

194
00:16:14.988 --> 00:16:22.824
I said, "Well I knew . . .” I said, "You know, in this life we cannot have everything we want.

195
00:16:22.824 --> 00:16:26.306
In this life, there's a give and take."

196
00:16:26.306 --> 00:16:34.576
And I said, "Do you think everything I did in college or high school was something I loved doing?"

197
00:16:34.576 --> 00:16:45.024
I said, "But I had a goal in mind, and I knew if I didn't do certain things, I wasn't going to make it."

198
00:16:45.024 --> 00:16:48.071
But she said, "I am so grateful."

199
00:16:48.071 --> 00:16:49.812
She said, "Guess what?"

200
00:16:49.812 --> 00:16:51.118
I said, "What?"

201
00:16:51.118 --> 00:16:55.035
"They hired me as the principal at this school."

202
00:16:55.035 --> 00:16:56.776
I said, "You see?"

203
00:16:56.776 --> 00:16:59.824
She said, "Because I spoke so well."

204
00:16:59.824 --> 00:17:02.000
I said, "Well congratulations."

205
00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:10.101
It's things like that for any teacher when your students come back and tell you how successful they have been,

206
00:17:10.101 --> 00:17:15.772
that in itself is worth any amount of money, because money is not everything.

207
00:17:15.772 --> 00:17:22.253
And I've had some students who have really done well, and, of course, they're grown now,

208
00:17:22.253 --> 00:17:30.354
they have families of their own, some of them live in the Lake Charles area, and they're doing really well.

209
00:17:30.354 --> 00:17:34.810
In any situation, everybody is not going to be as successful.

210
00:17:34.810 --> 00:17:37.646
And that . . . But they remember me [laughs].

211
00:17:37.646 --> 00:17:47.772
Oh, when I go somewhere, like I go to the store, some of them are working at Walmart, some of them are working at Lowe's.

212
00:17:47.772 --> 00:17:49.797
"Ms. Rogers, you remember me?"

213
00:17:49.797 --> 00:17:53.038
I say, "Well how can I forget you?"

214
00:17:53.038 --> 00:17:56.684
I said, "Now, what are you doing for yourself?"

215
00:17:56.684 --> 00:17:58.709
"Oh Ms. Rogers, I'm working."

216
00:17:58.709 --> 00:18:00.329
I said, "Well good.

217
00:18:00.329 --> 00:18:02.354
You have you a job."

218
00:18:02.354 --> 00:18:07.215
I said, "Hopefully you will be able to stay on that job."

219
00:18:07.215 --> 00:18:10.861
"Oh, I am, because I want to move up."

220
00:18:10.861 --> 00:18:13.696
I said, "Well you got the right

221
00:18:13.696 --> 00:18:14.101
<c.p>attitude."

222
00:18:14.101 --> 00:18:18.962
And those experiences, all of us like to see students do well.

223
00:18:18.962 --> 00:18:28.278
The sad thing, to me, is that many of them could do better than what they do, but they don't want the challenge.

224
00:18:28.278 --> 00:18:31.924
It's my way, or I just won't do it.

225
00:18:31.924 --> 00:18:38.000
I’m going to say this and then I'm going to move onto something else.

226
00:18:38.000 --> 00:18:46.537
I remember—this is after I had retired from Barbe—I worked at a school called . . . What was that school?

227
00:18:46.537 --> 00:18:47.486
Adult Education.

228
00:18:47.486 --> 00:18:50.332
But that wasn't the exact name.

229
00:18:50.332 --> 00:18:59.818
But the bottom line was the students that would come to that school, they were students who had dropped out,

230
00:18:59.818 --> 00:19:16.893
or either the young ladies had become pregnant and they had to get out of high school and come to that school in the evening in order to get enough credits to be able to graduate.

231
00:19:16.893 --> 00:19:22.584
So, I had this . . . There was a man, he was an adult,

232
00:19:22.584 --> 00:19:29.699
he came from my class and he said, "Ms. Rogers . . . " He talked to me alone.

233
00:19:29.699 --> 00:19:33.968
He said, "I want to learn how to read."

234
00:19:33.968 --> 00:19:41.557
I said, "Well let me tell you, I will do everything I can to help you."

235
00:19:41.557 --> 00:19:44.402
And he was just so conscientious.

236
00:19:44.402 --> 00:19:49.145
He was determined that he was going to make it.

237
00:19:49.145 --> 00:19:53.414
And I did what I could to help him.

238
00:19:53.414 --> 00:20:02.900
Sometimes he wanted to stay after the classes were over so that there would be certain things he could learn.

239
00:20:02.900 --> 00:20:10.015
And do you know, every time I think about it . . . Now this was an adult.

240
00:20:10.015 --> 00:20:12.860
He graduated from that high school.

241
00:20:12.860 --> 00:20:15.232
It was called the P.M.

242
00:20:15.232 --> 00:20:28.512
High School, but it was for the young adults who had dropped out for whatever reason and they would be able to come back and get their diploma.

243
00:20:28.512 --> 00:20:36.576
And this man, when he started the school he was already a family man, but he decided,

244
00:20:36.576 --> 00:20:45.113
he told me, that he was going to go back to school to get his twelfth grade diploma.

245
00:20:45.113 --> 00:20:56.496
So anyway, we worked, we worked, and the other students, they didn't like for me to take up a lot of time with him.

246
00:20:56.496 --> 00:21:06.457
But my attitude was, he doesn't know; you've been in school and you can read—because this man came there not reading.

247
00:21:06.457 --> 00:21:14.994
He was using books that was on a different grade level than what the regular students were using.

248
00:21:14.994 --> 00:21:20.211
But that man is now a minister, and he reads well,

249
00:21:20.211 --> 00:21:27.800
and I just . . . It's . . . You get gratification when you see an adult or a student so

250
00:21:27.800 --> 00:21:35.863
<c.p>conscientious that they will do whatever they need to do to learn what they need to learn.

251
00:21:35.863 --> 00:21:42.029
And there was another man in my class, he was in his seventies.

252
00:21:42.029 --> 00:21:45.824
He told me . . . He was a white guy.

253
00:21:45.824 --> 00:21:53.887
He said he was in high school at Lake Charles High School, but his parents needed help,

254
00:21:53.887 --> 00:22:02.899
so the only way he thought that he could help them was to volunteer and go into the service,

255
00:22:02.899 --> 00:22:10.962
because he would draw a check every month and he would be able to send money home.

256
00:22:10.962 --> 00:22:21.396
He said, "You know Mrs. Rogers, I told myself if I lived long enough, I was going to graduate from high school."

257
00:22:21.396 --> 00:22:27.562
So I said, "I tell you what, I am willing to help you."

258
00:22:27.562 --> 00:22:31.831
Of course, he could read and he could write.

259
00:22:31.831 --> 00:22:35.626
But he said, "I'm just so far behind."

260
00:22:35.626 --> 00:22:38.946
I said, "Well don't worry about that!"

261
00:22:38.946 --> 00:22:43.214
I said, "Where there's a will, there's a way."

262
00:22:43.214 --> 00:22:49.855
And I said, "When we are discussing something or doing something you don't understand,

263
00:22:49.855 --> 00:22:57.443
don't pretend . . . " I said, "Either you come to me personally, or you ask your questions openly."

264
00:22:57.443 --> 00:23:02.661
I tell you, some of those students could be so rude.

265
00:23:02.661 --> 00:23:05.981
And I said, "It's up to you."

266
00:23:05.981 --> 00:23:11.198
But that man came into that class, and he worked hard.

267
00:23:11.198 --> 00:23:19.261
I was just so proud of him, because he was the one who was determined to succeed.

268
00:23:19.261 --> 00:23:24.004
The night of graduation, he said, "Ms. Rogers, thank you.

269
00:23:24.004 --> 00:23:25.427
I can read."

270
00:23:25.427 --> 00:23:27.325
And you know what?

271
00:23:27.325 --> 00:23:31.119
I didn't want to cry in his presence.

272
00:23:31.119 --> 00:23:37.759
I was crying because, well tears were coming, because they were tears of joy.

273
00:23:37.759 --> 00:23:42.028
And most teachers like to see students do well.

274
00:23:42.028 --> 00:23:44.874
It doesn't matter about their age.

275
00:23:44.874 --> 00:23:52.937
If they want to learn, most teachers are willing to do what they can to help you.

276
00:23:52.937 --> 00:24:01.000
And I . . . Look, I could go on, and on, and on because I've had some situations.

277
00:24:01.000 --> 00:24:06.500
<v ARSENEAULT>I'm curious to know how you got the job at Mossville.

278
00:24:06.500 --> 00:24:10.000
<v ROGERS>Oh, how did I get the job?

279
00:24:10.000 --> 00:24:10.500
Okay.

280
00:24:10.500 --> 00:24:14.500
When I graduated from college . . . well, in college,

281
00:24:14.500 --> 00:24:23.500
they had told us if we . . . I was certified to become a teacher, some of the things we

282
00:24:23.500 --> 00:24:25.000
<c.p>needed to do.

283
00:24:25.000 --> 00:24:35.500
And they said that you could write a letter of application and they would guide you in the steps and everything.

284
00:24:35.500 --> 00:24:37.000
So, I did.

285
00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:47.500
I sent one to Calcasieu Parish, I sent one to East Baton Rouge, and West, one at Caddo in Shreveport [Louisiana].

286
00:24:47.500 --> 00:24:58.000
I didn't want to go to . . . I lived in a small town, and I wanted to go to a larger place.

287
00:24:58.000 --> 00:25:11.000
I never knew that Mossville existed, because when I wrote the letter, the letter didn't go to Mossville; it went to the Calcasieu Parish School Board.

288
00:25:11.000 --> 00:25:17.500
They were the . . . well, the school was just opening, so they needed teachers.

289
00:25:17.500 --> 00:25:23.500
Every person that they would hire would be new to that school.

290
00:25:23.500 --> 00:25:28.000
So I wrote the letter, as I said earlier.

291
00:25:28.000 --> 00:25:29.000
Excuse me.

292
00:25:29.000 --> 00:25:33.500
And they sent me some forms to fill out.

293
00:25:33.500 --> 00:25:42.500
In the . . . Well, they had written a letter asking me if I were really interested in the job,

294
00:25:42.500 --> 00:25:46.000
they would consider me for the job.

295
00:25:46.000 --> 00:25:59.000
They wanted to know when would be a good time for me to come to the school board, because they could explain certain things to me.

296
00:25:59.000 --> 00:26:01.000
And I did.

297
00:26:01.000 --> 00:26:06.537
Then when I came back home . . . when I went back home,

298
00:26:06.537 --> 00:26:14.088
I had, as I said, applied in Caddo and I applied in East Baton Rouge.

299
00:26:14.088 --> 00:26:18.619
I had gotten a letter from East Baton Rouge.

300
00:26:18.619 --> 00:26:29.190
At the time I . . . Well, my mother died when I was in high school, and my father was living in California.

301
00:26:29.190 --> 00:26:35.735
My grandparents didn't want us to be separated, meaning my brother and me.

302
00:26:35.735 --> 00:26:38.755
So they wanted to keep us.

303
00:26:38.755 --> 00:26:45.803
After I came from Lake Charles back to my home, which was in Arcadia,

304
00:26:45.803 --> 00:26:50.837
about fifty-five miles from Shreveport . . . You know where Shreveport is?

305
00:26:50.837 --> 00:26:56.878
Well anyway, I had gotten this letter from Baton Rouge considering me,

306
00:26:56.878 --> 00:27:03.925
because at the time we had to take . . . what kind of test was it?

307
00:27:03.925 --> 00:27:04.932
The ACT?

308
00:27:04.932 --> 00:27:13.993
But . . . No, you had to make a certain score within the subject area that you had majored in.

309
00:27:13.993 --> 00:27:20.537
They had seen my test scores, so they were offering me a position.

310
00:27:20.537 --> 00:27:27.082
I had not worked in Calcasieu Parish, but I had signed a contract.

311
00:27:27.082 --> 00:27:31.109
I was going to start in that August.

312
00:27:31.109 --> 00:27:42.687
So when my grandmother . . . She said, "Well, I don't see why you would go to Baton Rouge and you already have a job."

313
00:27:42.687 --> 00:27:45.707
You know, that kind of talk.

314
00:27:45.707 --> 00:27:48.728
I was always . . . I always tried

315
00:27:48.728 --> 00:27:50.238
<c.p>to be obedient.

316
00:27:50.238 --> 00:27:57.286
So, I said, "Well, Lord, I just guess I'll go on to Calcasieu Parish."

317
00:27:57.286 --> 00:28:00.306
And that's how I ended up.

318
00:28:00.306 --> 00:28:05.844
I got here . . . Well my cousin had worked in Calcasieu Parish,

319
00:28:05.844 --> 00:28:12.388
so when it was time for me to come down here to live,

320
00:28:12.388 --> 00:28:19.939
she knew a lady who had worked with her who lived in the Mossville area.

321
00:28:19.939 --> 00:28:29.000
She talked to this lady to see if I could stay with her, and the lady agreed.

322
00:28:29.000 --> 00:28:31.045
<v ARSENEAULT>Who was the lady?

323
00:28:31.045 --> 00:28:31.557
<v ROGERS>Pardon?

324
00:28:31.557 --> 00:28:34.625
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember the lady's name?

325
00:28:34.625 --> 00:28:36.670
<v ROGERS>What was her name?

326
00:28:36.670 --> 00:28:39.739
Ms. LeVan is her . . . Ms. LeVan.

327
00:28:39.739 --> 00:28:48.943
So she was working in Lake Charles, and I was going to be working over, across the lake.

328
00:28:48.943 --> 00:28:59.170
But anyway, she afforded me . . . she rented me a room, and I had access to the kitchen and the bathroom.

329
00:28:59.170 --> 00:28:59.682
So . . .

330
00:28:59.682 --> 00:29:02.239
<v ARSENEAULT>Where in Mossville was it?

331
00:29:02.239 --> 00:29:07.864
<v ROGERS>You know where the recreation center is where we go sometimes?

332
00:29:07.864 --> 00:29:10.420
It was across the street . . .

333
00:29:10.420 --> 00:29:10.932
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

334
00:29:10.932 --> 00:29:14.000
<v ROGERS>. . . further down to your right.

335
00:29:14.000 --> 00:29:16.500
<v ARSENEAULT>So not on, like, Charles [Street]?

336
00:29:16.500 --> 00:29:19.417
<v ROGERS>No, I wasn't . . . It was in Mossville.

337
00:29:19.417 --> 00:29:19.833
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

338
00:29:19.833 --> 00:29:24.000
<v ROGERS>You know the recreation building where we have dinner sometimes?

339
00:29:24.000 --> 00:29:24.417
<v ARSENEAULT>[Agrees]

340
00:29:24.417 --> 00:29:25.250
<v ROGERS>Okay, that's . . .

341
00:29:25.250 --> 00:29:29.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So it was off the Old Spanish Trail?

342
00:29:29.000 --> 00:29:31.500
<v ROGERS>It's on the Old Spanish Trail.

343
00:29:31.500 --> 00:29:31.917
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

344
00:29:31.917 --> 00:29:33.167
<v ROGERS>That's the rec.

345
00:29:33.167 --> 00:29:36.083
To your left, if you're going west . . .

346
00:29:36.083 --> 00:29:38.167
<v ARSENEAULT>It's like the railroad track.

347
00:29:38.167 --> 00:29:38.583
<v ROGERS>Yes.

348
00:29:38.583 --> 00:29:41.083
I [had?] across the railroad track.

349
00:29:41.083 --> 00:29:41.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

350
00:29:41.500 --> 00:29:54.000
<v ROGERS>And to my left there was a store in front of the house where the lady lived that I was living with . . . That I was going to live with.

351
00:29:54.000 --> 00:29:54.515
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

352
00:29:54.515 --> 00:29:56.061
Was that Garrett’s?

353
00:29:56.061 --> 00:29:56.576
<v ROGERS>Yes.

354
00:29:56.576 --> 00:30:00.697
We lived . . . Her house was behind that store.

355
00:30:00.697 --> 00:30:03.788
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it on the same property?

356
00:30:03.788 --> 00:30:06.364
<v ROGERS>No, it was her property.

357
00:30:06.364 --> 00:30:06.879
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

358
00:30:06.879 --> 00:30:08.939
<v ROGERS>It was her property.

359
00:30:08.939 --> 00:30:13.576
So I stayed there . . . oh, for a long time.

360
00:30:13.576 --> 00:30:19.758
I don't remember the number of years, but I did stay there.

361
00:30:19.758 --> 00:30:24.909
And then I didn't have transportation when I went there.

362
00:30:24.909 --> 00:30:26.970
<v ARSENEAULT>So you just walked?

363
00:30:26.970 --> 00:30:28.515
<v ROGERS>I walked sometimes.

364
00:30:28.515 --> 00:30:36.242
Sometimes parents would be bringing their students to school and they would pick me up.

365
00:30:36.242 --> 00:30:46.030
Then, I met a lady who was hired at Mossville High School during the time that I was hired.

366
00:30:46.030 --> 00:30:49.636
Her mother lived down in Bel Air.

367
00:30:49.636 --> 00:30:52.727
You ever heard of Bel Air?

368
00:30:52.727 --> 00:30:53.242
<c.p>Okay.

369
00:30:53.242 --> 00:30:56.333
Her mother lived in Bel Air.

370
00:30:56.333 --> 00:31:04.061
So she decided that she was going to build her some apartments in Bel Air,

371
00:31:04.061 --> 00:31:11.788
and she said, "If you want to, Gloria, I'll make sure you get a room."

372
00:31:11.788 --> 00:31:14.364
So I said, "Why not?"

373
00:31:14.364 --> 00:31:18.485
Because that would be closer to the school.

374
00:31:18.485 --> 00:31:21.061
So anyway, that's what happened.

375
00:31:21.061 --> 00:31:25.697
I left Mrs. LeVan . . . I don't remember the year.

376
00:31:25.697 --> 00:31:30.333
But anyway . . . Let's say school is out in May.

377
00:31:30.333 --> 00:31:36.000
I did not return to Mrs. LeVan the following school year.

378
00:31:36.000 --> 00:31:41.667
In fact, I stayed in my friend's apartment in Bel Air,

379
00:31:41.667 --> 00:31:51.455
and then after a while one of those plants wanted to buy them out and, of course, they did.

380
00:31:51.455 --> 00:31:55.061
So it was just . . . something, something, something.

381
00:31:55.061 --> 00:32:06.394
When we were told that the high school at Mossville was going to be disbanded, we were hurt because, like I said,

382
00:32:06.394 --> 00:32:19.788
our students would not be able to get the recognition that they would have gotten had they remained at least the remainder of that school year.

383
00:32:19.788 --> 00:32:21.848
But anyway, that's life.

384
00:32:21.848 --> 00:32:27.000
When we went to Westlake High, oh my God.

385
00:32:27.000 --> 00:32:30.264
<v ARSENEAULT>I definitely want to hear about that.

386
00:32:30.264 --> 00:32:32.596
But first, I'm so curious.

387
00:32:32.596 --> 00:32:35.861
What was your first impression of Mossville?

388
00:32:35.861 --> 00:32:41.923
Since you didn't grow up there, you got a really unique outsider's perspective.

389
00:32:41.923 --> 00:32:43.322
<v ROGERS>I surely did.

390
00:32:43.322 --> 00:32:48.452
When I went to Mossville I didn't know anybody over there.

391
00:32:48.452 --> 00:32:54.048
Now, the lady that I lived with, I didn't even know her.

392
00:32:54.048 --> 00:32:59.644
But my cousin knew her because they worked at the same school,

393
00:32:59.644 --> 00:33:07.106
and my cousin was responsible for helping me to be able to live with Ms. LeVan.

394
00:33:07.106 --> 00:33:12.702
The first time . . . Well, new and young, trying to find your way.

395
00:33:12.702 --> 00:33:22.029
I found out where the church was, the church for my faith, and some of the members would tell me,

396
00:33:22.029 --> 00:33:32.288
"If you want to go to church at Mount Zion, let us know and we would be glad to pick you up."

397
00:33:32.288 --> 00:33:36.019
I said, "Well great," I'm thinking to myself.

398
00:33:36.019 --> 00:33:38.817
So they said, "Just call us.

399
00:33:38.817 --> 00:33:42.548
We will be happy to pick you up."

400
00:33:42.548 --> 00:33:43.947
So I stepped

401
00:33:43.947 --> 00:33:46.279
<c.p>out on faith, believing them.

402
00:33:46.279 --> 00:33:49.543
And they did; they picked me up.

403
00:33:49.543 --> 00:33:59.803
They would see that I would get to church, and they would see that I get back to where I was living.

404
00:33:59.803 --> 00:34:04.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So you went to the Mount Zion Baptist?

405
00:34:04.000 --> 00:34:10.357
<v ROGERS>Yes, because the lady that I lived with, she was a staunch Catholic.

406
00:34:10.357 --> 00:34:18.181
Sometimes she would bring me to Lake Charles because she was a member at Sacred Heart,

407
00:34:18.181 --> 00:34:23.560
and sometimes she would bring me to Sacred Heart with her.

408
00:34:23.560 --> 00:34:32.363
Not that I knew anything about the Catholic religion, but I was gradually learning, because I was curious.

409
00:34:32.363 --> 00:34:43.121
By that time, by the time I had begun to really catch on, I had begun to get involved at Mount Zion,

410
00:34:43.121 --> 00:34:51.923
which meant I would spend my time at Mount Zion, rather than coming over here with my landlady.

411
00:34:51.923 --> 00:34:55.346
The people, I thought, were really nice.

412
00:34:55.346 --> 00:35:05.615
They really treated me nice, and they would tell me if I needed a ride to town, just let them know.

413
00:35:05.615 --> 00:35:09.038
I thought they were really, really nice.

414
00:35:09.038 --> 00:35:11.484
They made me feel welcomed.

415
00:35:11.484 --> 00:35:18.819
I had begun to like the area because all the people, they were so nice.

416
00:35:18.819 --> 00:35:24.687
I remember there was a lady who worked at Mossville High School.

417
00:35:24.687 --> 00:35:29.577
She liked to cook, but she lived in Lake Charles.

418
00:35:29.577 --> 00:35:34.467
She would say . . . Because at the time I wasn't married.

419
00:35:34.467 --> 00:35:39.846
She said, "Ms. Kilgore, you know I like to cook chicken."

420
00:35:39.846 --> 00:35:43.269
And she said, "Would you like some?"

421
00:35:43.269 --> 00:35:45.714
I said, "Oh, yes ma'am."

422
00:35:45.714 --> 00:35:52.071
And the question was, how was I going to get to Lake Charles?

423
00:35:52.071 --> 00:35:58.429
But I had some friends who had cars, and they would bring me.

424
00:35:58.429 --> 00:36:08.209
But it all worked out well, because when I went over across the lake I didn't know anybody over there.

425
00:36:08.209 --> 00:36:16.033
But after I stayed there a while, I began to fall in just like the others.

426
00:36:16.033 --> 00:36:19.456
The parents were cooperative with their children.

427
00:36:19.456 --> 00:36:29.725
I tell you, there were some boys in my class and I would stay on them and they didn't like that.

428
00:36:29.725 --> 00:36:31.192
I'll never forget.

429
00:36:31.192 --> 00:36:33.637
I had bought . . . After I

430
00:36:33.637 --> 00:36:44.885
<c.p>had worked there two or three years, I had begun to save my money so to have a down payment on my car.

431
00:36:44.885 --> 00:36:48.797
I bought a Ford Fairlane 500 from Sulphur.

432
00:36:48.797 --> 00:36:57.599
One of the men [Oliver Jones] who worked at Mossville High School, he's from somewhere around New Orleans.

433
00:36:57.599 --> 00:37:02.000
But anyway, he taught me how to drive.

434
00:37:02.000 --> 00:37:08.637
So these boys, sometimes they wouldn't do their work like they were supposed to have done.

435
00:37:08.637 --> 00:37:14.859
And because they didn't, they didn't get the grade they thought they should have gotten.

436
00:37:14.859 --> 00:37:21.910
But I didn't care, because at that time my attitude was "whatever" and they didn't like that.

437
00:37:21.910 --> 00:37:26.059
So I guess they were out to show me "whatever."

438
00:37:26.059 --> 00:37:27.718
So anyway, what happened?

439
00:37:27.718 --> 00:37:30.207
They did something to my car.

440
00:37:30.207 --> 00:37:33.110
I really don't remember what they did.

441
00:37:33.110 --> 00:37:35.599
But they messed up my car.

442
00:37:35.599 --> 00:37:39.747
I didn't . . . I had no idea who had done it.

443
00:37:39.747 --> 00:37:40.162
None.

444
00:37:40.162 --> 00:37:45.969
And one of the boys said, "Ms. Rogers, I want to talk to you."

445
00:37:45.969 --> 00:37:50.117
No, Ms. Kilgore, because at that time I wasn't married.

446
00:37:50.117 --> 00:37:54.680
So he said, "Do you know who messed up your car?"

447
00:37:54.680 --> 00:37:57.169
I said, "I have no idea."

448
00:37:57.169 --> 00:38:00.487
And he went on and he told me.

449
00:38:00.487 --> 00:38:03.806
So I said, "Well that's just too bad.

450
00:38:03.806 --> 00:38:06.294
I'm going to call the police."

451
00:38:06.294 --> 00:38:07.539
And I did.

452
00:38:07.539 --> 00:38:08.368
I called.

453
00:38:08.368 --> 00:38:14.176
I didn't consult their parents, because I didn't really know their parents that well.

454
00:38:14.176 --> 00:38:16.250
But I called the police.

455
00:38:16.250 --> 00:38:24.960
And the police . . . I don't remember what the police did, but the policemen did find them and he talked to them.

456
00:38:24.960 --> 00:38:29.108
I don't know if they had to pay a fine.

457
00:38:29.108 --> 00:38:31.597
I really don't know the details.

458
00:38:31.597 --> 00:38:33.671
It was so long ago.

459
00:38:33.671 --> 00:38:39.478
So one of the parents called me and she said . . . well, she identified herself.

460
00:38:39.478 --> 00:38:41.138
She said, "Ms. Kilgore,

461
00:38:41.138 --> 00:38:49.849
I understand that my brother or some relative had . . . was one of the boys who helped them mess up your car."

462
00:38:49.849 --> 00:38:51.508
I said, "Yes, ma'am.

463
00:38:51.508 --> 00:38:54.411
Another student told me who did it."

464
00:38:54.411 --> 00:39:01.048
And I said, "I believe that young man is trustworthy”, my point being I believed him.

465
00:39:01.048 --> 00:39:07.270
So she said, "I wish you would have called us before you called the police."

466
00:39:07.270 --> 00:39:09.759
You realize I didn't know that.

467
00:39:09.759 --> 00:39:18.055
You know how you're young in your first year, there's so much you don't know, but you learn through experiences.

468
00:39:18.055 --> 00:39:20.959
But anyways, and another parent called me.

469
00:39:20.959 --> 00:39:24.277
She said, "Oh, Ms. Rogers, I wish you

470
00:39:24.277 --> 00:39:27.181
<c.p>would have told me . . . told my husband."

471
00:39:27.181 --> 00:39:29.255
I said, "Ma'am, I'm sorry."

472
00:39:29.255 --> 00:39:32.573
I said, "I wasn't thinking along those lines."

473
00:39:32.573 --> 00:39:33.818
And I wasn't.

474
00:39:33.818 --> 00:39:38.380
But anyway, they had to pay to get my car fixed.

475
00:39:38.380 --> 00:39:42.114
I don't remember what all they had messed up.

476
00:39:42.114 --> 00:39:44.602
I know they messed it up.

477
00:39:44.602 --> 00:39:49.165
So their parents had them to come and apologize to me.

478
00:39:49.165 --> 00:39:53.728
I said to myself, "You said it because they're on you.

479
00:39:53.728 --> 00:39:55.802
You don't really mean it."

480
00:39:55.802 --> 00:39:58.706
But that . . . I just accepted their apologies.

481
00:39:58.706 --> 00:40:07.831
But I had no more problems after that because they saw that if you met a . . . if you bother . . . That's my territory.

482
00:40:07.831 --> 00:40:14.468
It would be the same if I’d go to your house and mess up your stuff.

483
00:40:14.468 --> 00:40:16.127
Then I'm in trouble.

484
00:40:16.127 --> 00:40:22.349
One of the boys told me, he said, "You know Ms. Rogers, sometimes I cry."

485
00:40:22.349 --> 00:40:24.423
I said, "Why you crying?"

486
00:40:24.423 --> 00:40:26.083
"About what I did."

487
00:40:26.083 --> 00:40:28.157
I said, "Well that's history."

488
00:40:28.157 --> 00:40:33.134
I said, "Well let me tell you, I hope it helped you.

489
00:40:33.134 --> 00:40:36.867
You wouldn't do that to somebody else, would you?"

490
00:40:36.867 --> 00:40:38.112
"Oh, no ma'am.

491
00:40:38.112 --> 00:40:38.941
No ma'am."

492
00:40:38.941 --> 00:40:43.090
I said, "So for you, it was a learning experience."

493
00:40:43.090 --> 00:40:46.408
But through it all, I weathered some storms.

494
00:40:46.408 --> 00:40:53.874
But when I look back, it was really a learning experience for me because when I left there,

495
00:40:53.874 --> 00:40:58.022
Mossville . . . not wanting to leave, but I needed to work.

496
00:40:58.022 --> 00:41:03.000
So I went to Westlake High, and of course another story.

497
00:41:03.000 --> 00:41:07.667
<v ARSENEAULT>So you were there when it first started until it got disbanded?

498
00:41:07.667 --> 00:41:08.444
<v ROGERS>That's correct.

499
00:41:08.444 --> 00:41:08.833
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

500
00:41:08.833 --> 00:41:10.778
So that was from 19 . . .

501
00:41:10.778 --> 00:41:15.056
<v ROGERS>1950 . . . I started at Mossville High School in August of 1955.

502
00:41:15.056 --> 00:41:17.000
<v ARSENEAULT>First class coming in.

503
00:41:17.000 --> 00:41:17.472
<v ROGERS>Yeah.

504
00:41:17.472 --> 00:41:27.856
It was an elementary . . . It was called the Mossville High School, but there was an elementary division, too, grades one through—what?—six, seven.

505
00:41:27.856 --> 00:41:30.688
And then . . . What did they have?

506
00:41:30.688 --> 00:41:35.408
The elementary, the middle school, and then the high school.

507
00:41:35.408 --> 00:41:38.712
High school was from ninth through twelfth.

508
00:41:38.712 --> 00:41:43.904
So the high school was disbanded after this integration came about.

509
00:41:43.904 --> 00:41:47.680
Now, it depended on where the students lived.

510
00:41:47.680 --> 00:41:58.536
If the students lived in a certain vicinity, those who were coming to Mossville High School, they would end up going to Sulphur.

511
00:41:58.536 --> 00:42:03.256
And then if the students lived in a certain area . . .

512
00:42:03.256 --> 00:42:05.144
<v ARSENEAULT>They’d go to Westlake.

513
00:42:05.144 --> 00:42:07.032
<v ROGERS>. . . they'd go to Westlake.

514
00:42:07.032 --> 00:42:09.864
They would go to Westlake High.

515
00:42:09.864 --> 00:42:15.056
And then the children from Vinton, they just stayed in Vinton.

516
00:42:15.056 --> 00:42:16.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

517
00:42:16.000 --> 00:42:25.059
<v ROGERS>They didn't have to come back to . . . Well the high school kids didn't because there was no high school after . . . let's see.

518
00:42:25.059 --> 00:42:26.294
That was 1955.

519
00:42:26.294 --> 00:42:26.706
No . . .

520
00:42:26.706 --> 00:42:27.941
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it '69?

521
00:42:27.941 --> 00:42:28.765
<v ROGERS>No, 1969.

522
00:42:28.765 --> 00:42:29.176
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

523
00:42:29.176 --> 00:42:32.059
<v ROGERS>That's when the high school was disbanded.

524
00:42:32.059 --> 00:42:34.941
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about the protests?

525
00:42:34.941 --> 00:42:37.000
Were there any protests?

526
00:42:37.000 --> 00:42:42.980
<v ROGERS>The only protest I saw was at school at Westlake High School.

527
00:42:42.980 --> 00:42:45.971
I don't remember the parents protesting.

528
00:42:45.971 --> 00:42:47.466
I don't remember.

529
00:42:47.466 --> 00:42:50.456
Our protest was at the school.

530
00:42:50.456 --> 00:42:56.935
Our students-- and when I say our, the black students--like I said earlier,

531
00:42:56.935 --> 00:43:08.896
they didn't want to leave because some of the guys played on the football team and they had certain positions that they would play,

532
00:43:08.896 --> 00:43:10.889
and they liked football.

533
00:43:10.889 --> 00:43:17.368
But going to another school that's already in progress, what do you do?

534
00:43:17.368 --> 00:43:22.850
I mean, you may be able to get on the team.

535
00:43:22.850 --> 00:43:26.837
I really don't know about the football set-up.

536
00:43:26.837 --> 00:43:33.316
But there were students who were in various activities at Mossville High School.

537
00:43:33.316 --> 00:43:38.300
They couldn't just go to Westlake High and get involved.

538
00:43:38.300 --> 00:43:42.287
They had to show that they were worthy.

539
00:43:42.287 --> 00:43:46.772
And some of the students were really, really upset.

540
00:43:46.772 --> 00:43:55.244
The first year, I would come home, I would tell my husband, I said, "You know what?

541
00:43:55.244 --> 00:44:02.221
I don't know if it's worth it," because I would be so stressed out.

542
00:44:02.221 --> 00:44:13.186
Stressed because . . . I don't know if you've ever been in this situation where you're not wanted, but yet you got to work.

543
00:44:13.186 --> 00:44:15.678
So what do you do?

544
00:44:15.678 --> 00:44:23.153
If I'd had a choice, I wouldn't have gone there; but the choice wasn't mine.

545
00:44:23.153 --> 00:44:31.127
But even though there were those faculty members at Westlake High who were really, really cooperative,

546
00:44:31.127 --> 00:44:42.590
because they understood we weren't there because we wanted to be there; we were there because our children need to get an education.

547
00:44:42.590 --> 00:44:49.068
And that was the high school that many of them were assigned to,

548
00:44:49.068 --> 00:44:54.550
so we just . . . But some of the teachers . . . I don't know.

549
00:44:54.550 --> 00:44:58.039
And I felt so sorry for the

550
00:44:58.039 --> 00:45:00.531
<c.p>principal because . . . Oh my God.

551
00:45:00.531 --> 00:45:07.010
He was trying to do the best that he could considering the situation.

552
00:45:07.010 --> 00:45:10.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember his name?

553
00:45:10.000 --> 00:45:11.470
<v ROGERS>[Agrees] Mr. Latard.

554
00:45:11.470 --> 00:45:14.411
To me, he was so gentle.

555
00:45:14.411 --> 00:45:23.723
He was trying to get those teachers who didn't want us there to understand this is not his doing.

556
00:45:23.723 --> 00:45:29.115
<v ARSENEAULT>How did you know the other teachers didn't want you there?

557
00:45:29.115 --> 00:45:31.565
<v ROGERS>By the way they acted.

558
00:45:31.565 --> 00:45:34.016
They never told us anything.

559
00:45:34.016 --> 00:45:40.387
Well, they never told me and I don't think they told anybody else.

560
00:45:40.387 --> 00:45:42.348
But you know what?

561
00:45:42.348 --> 00:45:44.308
<v ARSENEAULT>It was their attitude.

562
00:45:44.308 --> 00:45:44.798
<v ROGERS>Yeah.

563
00:45:44.798 --> 00:45:50.680
Certain teachers were really nice and they would . . . because . . . To my left,

564
00:45:50.680 --> 00:45:55.091
there was this teacher who taught twelfth grade English.

565
00:45:55.091 --> 00:46:01.953
To my right, there was this teacher who taught tenth and eleventh grade English.

566
00:46:01.953 --> 00:46:02.443
Okay.

567
00:46:02.443 --> 00:46:12.735
When I went there, I had twelfth grade English, I had tenth grade English, and I had eleventh grade English--three preparations.

568
00:46:12.735 --> 00:46:15.186
But that didn't bother me.

569
00:46:15.186 --> 00:46:24.008
The lady to my right, she said, "Ms. Rogers,” she said, “I teach tenth and eleventh grade English."

570
00:46:24.008 --> 00:46:31.360
And she said, "If there's any way I can help you, just let me know."

571
00:46:31.360 --> 00:46:33.320
And I believed her.

572
00:46:33.320 --> 00:46:34.791
She was sincere.

573
00:46:34.791 --> 00:46:39.692
But the one to my left, she didn't say anything.

574
00:46:39.692 --> 00:46:45.083
I mean, not that it bothered me, but . . . I don't know.

575
00:46:45.083 --> 00:46:50.474
Some days . . . well, after we got there the teachers had to

576
00:46:50.474 --> 00:46:58.316
<c.p>stand on the hall to see . . . make sure there weren't . . . there would not be any disturbances.

577
00:46:58.316 --> 00:46:59.787
And Oh, Lord.

578
00:46:59.787 --> 00:47:04.198
The first week we were there, it was horrific.

579
00:47:04.198 --> 00:47:09.589
And when I would come home, I would just . . . Oh, Lord.

580
00:47:09.589 --> 00:47:14.000
All I could do was just sit down.

581
00:47:14.000 --> 00:47:17.134
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of stuff would you see?

582
00:47:17.134 --> 00:47:22.059
<v ROGERS>Well, it wasn't so much . . . Well, I didn't see a fight,

583
00:47:22.059 --> 00:47:28.774
but I was told that there was just fighting all up and down the hall.

584
00:47:28.774 --> 00:47:32.803
Because once the bell would ring, the tardy bell,

585
00:47:32.803 --> 00:47:40.862
I would have the students come in and I would . . . my door would be . . . when I closed it,

586
00:47:40.862 --> 00:47:45.787
it would be locked so they couldn't come into the room.

587
00:47:45.787 --> 00:47:48.921
I just tried to tune them out.

588
00:47:48.921 --> 00:47:55.636
And then there were students in the room, "Ms. Rogers, why you closing that door?"

589
00:47:55.636 --> 00:47:58.770
I said, "You don't have any idea?"

590
00:47:58.770 --> 00:48:07.724
I didn't even explain because my thinking was: they were intelligent enough to know why I was closing the door.

591
00:48:07.724 --> 00:48:15.335
I didn't explain, and I said, "Come on students, we have to get on with this class.

592
00:48:15.335 --> 00:48:16.678
We have to.

593
00:48:16.678 --> 00:48:21.155
We don't have any choice because we are already behind."

594
00:48:21.155 --> 00:48:29.661
And then we would go to the cafeteria and somebody would end up hitting somebody, that kind of foolishness.

595
00:48:29.661 --> 00:48:31.900
To me, it was foolishness.

596
00:48:31.900 --> 00:48:34.138
But the students were angry.

597
00:48:34.138 --> 00:48:36.824
They were angry on both sides.

598
00:48:36.824 --> 00:48:41.301
The black students were angry, the white students were angry.

599
00:48:41.301 --> 00:48:43.092
Not all of them.

600
00:48:43.092 --> 00:48:47.121
Some of the white students, they were really nice.

601
00:48:47.121 --> 00:48:50.703
They tried to make the students feel welcomed.

602
00:48:50.703 --> 00:48:56.075
Oh, Lord, but some of them . . . no way, no way, no way.

603
00:48:56.075 --> 00:49:01.000
But it's amazing how time can bring about a change.

604
00:49:01.000 --> 00:49:08.185
My first year there we had one . . . well, when we went there Mr. Latard was the principal.

605
00:49:08.185 --> 00:49:12.834
He had to give it up before the year was over.

606
00:49:12.834 --> 00:49:14.948
Then they hired another man.

607
00:49:14.948 --> 00:49:17.061
I don't remember his name.

608
00:49:17.061 --> 00:49:20.442
And then he had to give it up.

609
00:49:20.442 --> 00:49:25.091
Well I’m not saying he had to, but I guess it

610
00:49:25.091 --> 00:49:26.782
<c.p>was so much commotion.

611
00:49:26.782 --> 00:49:29.318
You got to have some peace.

612
00:49:29.318 --> 00:49:30.163
He quit.

613
00:49:30.163 --> 00:49:36.503
And then they got a third person, and that person stayed while I was there.

614
00:49:36.503 --> 00:49:37.348
He stayed.

615
00:49:37.348 --> 00:49:40.729
He finished the remainder of the school year.

616
00:49:40.729 --> 00:49:45.378
I was there for three or four years at Westlake High.

617
00:49:45.378 --> 00:49:53.831
And then this was when . . . at the time my husband was stationed after . . . Wright-Patterson Air Force Base in Dayton, Ohio.

618
00:49:53.831 --> 00:49:56.367
So I had gone up there.

619
00:49:56.367 --> 00:50:05.666
My children . . . Well the three of us—I had a boy and a girl—we went there to be with him for the summer.

620
00:50:05.666 --> 00:50:14.964
And then I got this telegram from Mr. Russell who, at the time, was the superintendent of the Calcasieu Parish School System.

621
00:50:14.964 --> 00:50:21.304
He was telling me to please call his office and he gave me the time.

622
00:50:21.304 --> 00:50:22.994
And that I did.

623
00:50:22.994 --> 00:50:28.066
First time I called I didn't get him because he wasn't in.

624
00:50:28.066 --> 00:50:31.870
But I did call and I did get him.

625
00:50:31.870 --> 00:50:33.561
He said, "Mrs. Rogers?"

626
00:50:33.561 --> 00:50:34.829
I said, "Yes."

627
00:50:34.829 --> 00:50:42.014
He said, "I'm calling because we are moving you from Westlake High School to Barbe High School."

628
00:50:42.014 --> 00:50:43.282
I said, "Why?"

629
00:50:43.282 --> 00:50:46.663
I don't even remember the excuse he gave.

630
00:50:46.663 --> 00:50:50.044
But he said, "You'll like it at Barbe.

631
00:50:50.044 --> 00:50:51.312
It's air conditioned."

632
00:50:51.312 --> 00:50:54.693
I said, "Sir, it's air conditioned over here."

633
00:50:54.693 --> 00:50:56.807
So that wasn't anything extra.

634
00:50:56.807 --> 00:50:59.765
He said, "But you would like Barbe."

635
00:50:59.765 --> 00:51:03.569
That was the first year of Barbe High School.

636
00:51:03.569 --> 00:51:06.950
And he said, "Do you know John Nicosia?”

637
00:51:06.950 --> 00:51:12.022
I said, "I've heard the name, but I don't really know him."

638
00:51:12.022 --> 00:51:17.517
And then he was telling me when I could come and meet him.

639
00:51:17.517 --> 00:51:21.743
He asked me if I knew where Barbe High was.

640
00:51:21.743 --> 00:51:24.279
I said, "No, I don't know."

641
00:51:24.279 --> 00:51:34.000
Anyway, during the course of the time that we were to meet I found out, but I didn't know where it was.

642
00:51:34.000 --> 00:51:35.152
<v ARSENEAULT>Where was it?

643
00:51:35.152 --> 00:51:38.225
<v ROGERS>It's on . . . What's the name of that street?

644
00:51:38.225 --> 00:51:40.913
Let's see . . . You heard of Nelson Road?

645
00:51:40.913 --> 00:51:41.297
<v ARSENEAULT>[Agrees]

646
00:51:41.297 --> 00:51:41.681
<v ROGERS>Okay.

647
00:51:41.681 --> 00:51:43.601
You go down Nelson . . . Well,

648
00:51:43.601 --> 00:51:52.435
at the time we were going on the interstate because I had some . . . There were three of us who were working at Barbe.

649
00:51:52.435 --> 00:51:56.659
My friend down the street, she was in the social studies,

650
00:51:56.659 --> 00:52:02.804
and then I had another friend who was in business education, and I was in English.

651
00:52:02.804 --> 00:52:04.341
So we would alternate.

652
00:52:04.341 --> 00:52:08.565
I would drive one week, and they would drive one week.

653
00:52:08.565 --> 00:52:17.783
But the bottom line is we would get on the interstate and we would get off on Nelson Road and make a left turn,

654
00:52:17.783 --> 00:52:27.000
and we would go all the way down to McNeese Street, West McNeese, and then you make a right, and there is Barbe.

655
00:52:27.000 --> 00:52:29.024
<v ARSENEAULT>So it's in Lake Charles?

656
00:52:29.024 --> 00:52:31.048
<v ROGERS>Yes, it's in Lake Charles.

657
00:52:31.048 --> 00:52:33.881
That was supposed to be the school.

658
00:52:33.881 --> 00:52:34.690
<v ARSENEAULT>I see.

659
00:52:34.690 --> 00:52:35.500
<v ROGERS>The school.

660
00:52:35.500 --> 00:52:39.548
There are those who still think it is the school.

661
00:52:39.548 --> 00:52:47.238
But anyway, students used to tell me, "Ms. Rogers . . ." [laughs] They, the students who would be talking to me,

662
00:52:47.238 --> 00:52:55.333
they had gone to Welsh Middle School, but then after they moved on up in grade they came to Barbe.

663
00:52:55.333 --> 00:52:59.381
They would tell me that, "Ms. Rogers, you know so-and-so?"

664
00:52:59.381 --> 00:53:07.071
Of course I didn't know who they were talking about, but they were trying to make me know them.

665
00:53:07.071 --> 00:53:09.905
I said, "No, I don't know them."

666
00:53:09.905 --> 00:53:11.929
"Well we ran her away."

667
00:53:11.929 --> 00:53:15.976
And they would tell me the things they would do.

668
00:53:15.976 --> 00:53:18.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Trying to scare you?

669
00:53:18.000 --> 00:53:18.455
<v ROGERS>Yes!

670
00:53:18.455 --> 00:53:22.091
I said, "Now wait, let's get something straight."

671
00:53:22.091 --> 00:53:28.909
I said, "Now if I leave it won't be because you will run me off.

672
00:53:28.909 --> 00:53:32.091
I'm leaving because I desire to leave."

673
00:53:32.091 --> 00:53:35.273
"Oh, but we would run you off.”

674
00:53:35.273 --> 00:53:38.455
I said, "I'm not worrying about that.

675
00:53:38.455 --> 00:53:42.091
I'm not worrying about your running me off."

676
00:53:42.091 --> 00:53:48.455
But anyway, once I got my first year there at Barbe, people were nice.

677
00:53:48.455 --> 00:53:50.273
They were really nice.

678
00:53:50.273 --> 00:53:58.000
After I set those students straight about what they had done to another teacher, they just said,

679
00:53:58.000 --> 00:54:06.182
"Well, I guess I may as well just fall in line and do what I got to do."

680
00:54:06.182 --> 00:54:08.000
They were cooperative.

681
00:54:08.000 --> 00:54:11.492
They used to tease me about my car.

682
00:54:11.492 --> 00:54:18.038
They said, "Ms. Rogers, I can't believe . . .” What did they call me . . . call my car?

683
00:54:18.038 --> 00:54:19.347
"The Green Machine."

684
00:54:19.347 --> 00:54:21.093
I said, "Oh yes."

685
00:54:21.093 --> 00:54:27.640
And they would ask me so many silly questions, questions I couldn't respond to like,

686
00:54:27.640 --> 00:54:34.623
"Ms. Rogers . . ." Now if we had read a story and we would be discussing that story,

687
00:54:34.623 --> 00:54:40.733
they would try to correlate it with some experience out there in the community.

688
00:54:40.733 --> 00:54:47.280
"Mrs. Rogers, why in north Lake Charles . . ." That's where a lot of black people live.

689
00:54:47.280 --> 00:54:51.644
"Why in north Lake Charles you see people with Cadillacs?"

690
00:54:51.644 --> 00:54:54.699
I said, "I can't answer that question."

691
00:54:54.699 --> 00:54:58.627
I said, "I don't live in north Lake Charles,

692
00:54:58.627 --> 00:55:08.229
so you need to talk with one of the persons who has a Cadillac and they might be able to tell you."

693
00:55:08.229 --> 00:55:11.284
I said, "But I can't tell you."

694
00:55:11.284 --> 00:55:15.212
And I knew they weren't going to do that.

695
00:55:15.212 --> 00:55:18.267
They would say crazy things like that.

696
00:55:18.267 --> 00:55:24.814
Sometimes I would get tickled; sometimes I would not depending on how they would respond.

697
00:55:24.814 --> 00:55:28.742
But there were some smart students in that class.

698
00:55:28.742 --> 00:55:37.034
But just like any student, they wouldn't work hard at all until they saw that they had to work.

699
00:55:37.034 --> 00:55:45.326
You're not here in school to come and play around; you're here to learn all that you can learn.

700
00:55:45.326 --> 00:55:49.691
And even at that, you still have not learned enough.

701
00:55:49.691 --> 00:55:51.000
But anyway.

702
00:55:51.000 --> 00:55:51.792
<v ARSENEAULT>I'm curious.

703
00:55:51.792 --> 00:55:55.755
We're almost at an hour and I would hate to . . .

704
00:55:55.755 --> 00:55:56.547
<v ROGERS>Okay, good.

705
00:55:56.547 --> 00:55:57.736
<v ARSENEAULT>. . . tire you out.

706
00:55:57.736 --> 00:55:58.528
<v ROGERS>Okay, good.

707
00:55:58.528 --> 00:55:59.321
<v ARSENEAULT>I know.

708
00:55:59.321 --> 00:56:00.113
<v ROGERS>Oh, Lord.

709
00:56:00.113 --> 00:56:02.094
<v ARSENEAULT>I have lots of questions . . .

710
00:56:02.094 --> 00:56:02.491
<v ROGERS>Okay.

711
00:56:02.491 --> 00:56:06.453
<v ARSENEAULT>. . . so I might have to come back for another interview.

712
00:56:06.453 --> 00:56:06.849
<v ROGERS>Okay.

713
00:56:06.849 --> 00:56:12.000
<v ARSENEAULT>But I wanted to ask you what Mossville means to you personally.

714
00:56:12.000 --> 00:56:18.997
<v ROGERS>I'm going to get to that answer—I'm going to give you an answer—

715
00:56:18.997 --> 00:56:21.688
<c.p>because I'm going to digress.

716
00:56:21.688 --> 00:56:30.837
My husband could not understand why I would not move my letter from that church, Mount Zion.

717
00:56:30.837 --> 00:56:43.216
And like I told him, Rogers, "This church, to me, is home," because when I came here shortly after I had been here,

718
00:56:43.216 --> 00:56:50.213
I united with that church, and I have seen people come and go.

719
00:56:50.213 --> 00:56:52.904
But each to his own.

720
00:56:52.904 --> 00:57:05.282
Like some people would say certain things, but I mean I guess they were justified in what they said or what they did.

721
00:57:05.282 --> 00:57:07.435
But that's that person.

722
00:57:07.435 --> 00:57:09.050
That wasn't me.

723
00:57:09.050 --> 00:57:18.199
My husband, as I said earlier, he couldn't understand why I would want to stay over there.

724
00:57:18.199 --> 00:57:23.043
We lived in Lake Charles and . . . because . . . I said,

725
00:57:23.043 --> 00:57:30.578
"Rogers,” I said, “why don't you come and unite with me at Mount Zion?"

726
00:57:30.578 --> 00:57:39.728
Well, in no uncertain terms he said, "No, I'm going to unite with the church over here."

727
00:57:39.728 --> 00:57:45.648
I said, "Well Rogers . . .” He said, "But I still don't understand."

728
00:57:45.648 --> 00:57:48.877
I said, "Well Rogers, that's home.

729
00:57:48.877 --> 00:57:51.030
That's home to me."

730
00:57:51.030 --> 00:57:59.641
When I first came over in that area, really I didn't even know that place existed.

731
00:57:59.641 --> 00:58:07.176
But after I united at that church, the people were very nice to me.

732
00:58:07.176 --> 00:58:09.867
They treated me with respect.

733
00:58:09.867 --> 00:58:14.711
I consider that home, even though realistically it isn't.

734
00:58:14.711 --> 00:58:20.631
But that's my attitude about it, because God has blessed me.

735
00:58:20.631 --> 00:58:28.704
I started out working with x-number of teachers, and many of those teachers are gone.

736
00:58:28.704 --> 00:58:30.857
Some younger, some older.

737
00:58:30.857 --> 00:58:36.777
So I'm thinking, well God maybe has a purpose for me.

738
00:58:36.777 --> 00:58:42.159
You don't know what God has in store for you.

739
00:58:42.159 --> 00:58:54.000
But for some reason I decided that I've been here this long, why am I going to unite with another church?

740
00:58:54.000 --> 00:59:03.202
So to respond to your question, the area, the Mossville area, to me, is like I'm going home.

741
00:59:03.202 --> 00:59:11.381
When I go over there, it's not that I like everything that I am subjected to.

742
00:59:11.381 --> 00:59:18.538
But you could be in your own house and not be happy about everything.

743
00:59:18.538 --> 00:59:19.561
That's life.

744
00:59:19.561 --> 00:59:26.717
But you live to adapt to--that's my attitude--the environments in which you find yourself.

745
00:59:26.717 --> 00:59:31.318
And if you like people, people will like you.

746
00:59:31.318 --> 00:59:37.453
If you don't like this or that about somebody, that's your problem.

747
00:59:37.453 --> 00:59:43.587
I'm not going to be affected by . . . because you don't like Mary.

748
00:59:43.587 --> 00:59:48.188
Don't try to get me to this light, Mary.

749
00:59:48.188 --> 00:59:53.300
<c.p>That's between you and Mary, not between Mary and me.

750
00:59:53.300 --> 01:00:00.457
The older I get, the more absorbed I become in God's way of life.

751
01:00:00.457 --> 01:00:08.126
I don't care if I'm black, I don't care if I'm brown, it doesn't matter.

752
01:00:08.126 --> 01:00:17.839
But one thing I do know, I cannot go around disliking people because of the color of their skin.

753
01:00:17.839 --> 01:00:22.951
If I do, I don't have a place in heaven.

754
01:00:22.951 --> 01:00:34.197
If it's rough down here now, can you imagine what it would be like if you die and your soul is lost?

755
01:00:34.197 --> 01:00:40.332
You go to hell, and you would be burning eternally. [phone rings]

756
01:00:40.332 --> 01:00:43.910
<v ARSENEAULT>Well that's a perfect place to pause.

757
01:00:43.910 --> 01:00:44.422
<v ROGERS>Huh?

758
01:00:44.422 --> 01:00:48.000
<v ARSENEAULT>That's a perfect place to stop.

759
01:00:48.000 --> 01:00:48.429
<c.p>[End Tape 4530.

760
01:00:48.429 --> 01:00:48.857
End Session II.]
